// #4203: THE ONE WORD A MUTATOR READS AT A BACK EDGE.
//
// Before this file, both backends open-coded `stwPollNeeded`'s whole predicate
// at every loop back edge: eighteen instructions, ten loads, three of them
// `ldar` (an acquire load is a barrier on AArch64, not merely a load), asking
// three independent questions — is a stop-the-world pending, is a preempt
// pending, has the GC trigger been crossed — of state the runtime had already
// computed for itself. The mutator paid to re-derive, every iteration, an
// answer that is "no" for essentially the whole life of a program.
//
// It now reads THIS word instead, and nothing else: load the cached address,
// acquire-load the word, branch if it is zero. Which of the three conditions
// fired is decided by `bit_rt_safepoint`'s own slow path, on the rare back edge
// where the word is set.
//
// SENSE: 0 means "nobody has asked for a safepoint"; nonzero means "take the
// slow path and find out why". The slow path re-derives every condition from
// the live state, so a spurious nonzero costs one wasted call and nothing else
// — `stwPollOn` already returns without acting when no clause holds, which is
// what makes a summary safe here at all. A missed nonzero is the only real
// failure, so every writer below publishes AFTER the state it summarises and
// every consumer clears BEFORE reading that state.
//
// THE INVARIANT, and why zero at process start is correct rather than a
// convenient default: this word is 0 exactly while no reason to poll has ever
// been published and none is currently pending. At time zero no subsystem is
// bound — `worldAddr` and `gcAddr` are 0, no worker exists, nothing is
// allocated — so there is no reason to publish, and a back-edge poll in that
// window is a no-op by construction (`stwPollOn` returns at its own `waddr ==
// 0` guard). Each of the three reasons publishes its own request the moment it
// can first exist: `worldRendezvous` (../gc/gcworldstop.bit) when it raises the
// stop flag, `sysmonTick` (./preempt.bit) when it flags a worker, `allocObject`
// (../gc/gcalloc.bit) when an allocation crosses `gcShouldCollect`, and
// `gcConfigure` (../gc/gc.bit) when the collector's configuration — including
// `BIT_GC=stress`, whose whole meaning is "poll at every back edge" — is
// installed. Pre-boot is not a special case; it is the general invariant
// holding at t=0.
//
// WHY A SEPARATE WORD AND NOT `anyRequested` (./preempt.bit), which the poll
// guard already cached: that word's clearing path, `refreshPreemptAny`, rescans
// the preempt slots and writes the answer. Widening its meaning to cover GC and
// stop-the-world would make that rescan clear a pending collection, and it
// cannot be taught otherwise without `runtime/sched` importing `runtime/gc` —
// the reverse of the edge that exists (E0044). Two words, each with one owner.
//
// WHY `runtime/sched` AND NOT `runtime/gc`: `runtime/gc`, `runtime/stw` and
// `runtime/root` all import `../sched`, and `../sched` imports none of them, so
// this is the only module every writer can reach.
//
// A [1]i64 rather than a bare `let w: i64` for the same reason every other
// global in this module is an array: `ptrOf` is how a `@nosplit` body addresses
// module state without indexing (E0075).
let pollAttention: [1]i64

// The address of `pollAttention`, for the one caller that needs it and cannot
// reach it any other way: BOTH backends' back-edge poll guard
// (`compiler/arm64call.bit`'s emitPollGuard, `compiler/x64controlflow.bit`'s
// xEmitPollGuard) fetches it once per polling function's ENTRY into that
// function's frame, then reads the word directly on every back edge. Module
// state cannot be pinned across the compiler/runtime object boundary — only a
// FUNCTION can (SPEC §11.9) — so an accessor is the only spelling available,
// exactly as `preemptAnyAddr` below it and `rootGcAddr`/`rootWorldAddr` are for
// theirs.
//
// A true leaf: no allocation, no park, no safepoint, and the address is a
// per-process constant for the whole life of the program, which is what makes
// caching it at function entry sound.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_poll_attention_addr") fn pollAttentionAddr(): int {
  return int(ptrOf(pollAttention))
}

// Publish "somebody wants a safepoint". MUST be called AFTER the store that
// makes the underlying condition true, never before: the reader's acquire load
// of this word is the acquire half of a message-passing pair whose release half
// is this sequentially-consistent store, so a mutator that observes the request
// is guaranteed to observe the state that caused it.
//
// Only ever SETS. No writer clears this word — see `pollConsume` for the one
// path that does, and why it does it first rather than last.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_poll_request") fn pollRequest() {
  atomicStore(ptrOf(pollAttention), 1)
}

// Consume the request, BEFORE reading any of the state it summarises.
//
// THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE CORRECTNESS ARGUMENT and it is the mirror image of
// `refreshPreemptAny`'s. A slow path that did its work and then cleared could
// lose a request published in between; clearing first cannot, because a writer
// stores its state before it stores this word:
//
//   - if the writer's state store lands before the consumer's subsequent reads,
//     the consumer sees the state and handles it;
//   - if it lands after them, then the writer's `pollRequest` — which follows
//     its own state store — lands after this clear, so the word ends nonzero
//     and the next back edge polls again.
//
// There is no interleaving that leaves the word 0 with a condition pending.
// This rests on the consumer's clear being ordered before its own subsequent
// atomic reads, which is what sequential consistency gives (`stlr` then `ldar`
// on AArch64 is an ordered pair; x86-64 TSO orders it through the store's
// implicit fence).
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_poll_consume") fn pollConsume() {
  atomicStore(ptrOf(pollAttention), 0)
}
